# Research Agent — Web Search + Report Generation

Part of my Gen AI learning journey — Phase 4: Agents.

## What this does
Give it a topic, and it searches the web on its own, reads through the results, and writes a clean, organized summary report — without me having to search or read anything manually.

## How it works (the Agent Loop)
1. **Act** — Calls a web search tool (DuckDuckGo, free, no API key) to fetch real, current results on the given topic
2. **Observe** — Reads through the returned search results (titles, snippets, sources)
3. **Think & Answer** — Synthesizes all the retrieved information into a structured report (title, key findings, conclusion) — using only what was found, not the model's own training knowledge

## Why this matters
My local model (Llama 3.2) has a training cutoff — it has no idea what happened in 2026. By giving it a web search **tool**, it fetched real, current information first, then generated a report using only that real data. This is the core idea behind AI Agents: not a smarter model, just a regular LLM given the ability to look things up before answering.

## Example run
**Topic:** "latest AI trends 2026"

**Report generated (summarized):**
- Open-source AI ecosystem is accelerating (IBM Granite, Ai2 Olmo 3, DeepSeek models)
- Experts predict the AI bubble may deflate in 2026, impacting the broader economy
- Multimodal agent workflows becoming mainstream (e.g. Moonshot AI's Kimi K2.5)
- AI playing a bigger role in real scientific discovery (physics, chemistry, biology)
- Growth of "AI factory" infrastructure for enterprise AI adoption

The agent correctly synthesized 4 separate search results into one coherent report, staying grounded in the actual retrieved content.

## Tools used
- Ollama + Llama 3.2 (free, local) — for reasoning and report generation
- `ddgs` (DuckDuckGo Search) — free web search, no API key required

## How to run
```bash
pip install ddgs ollama
ollama pull llama3.2
python research_agent.py
```

